.TH CBMAP_BEGIN_ITER 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cbmap_begin_iter \- create an iterator positioned at an ordered map's first entry
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cmap_iterator *cbmap_begin_iter(cbmap cbm, char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates an iterator at the first element of the map. Each step yields
the key and the value of one element, which you read through the common
accessors ccol_iter_key_ptr(3) and ccol_iter_val_ptr(3). The value pointer
points into the map's own storage, so writing through it updates the
stored value in place.

The usual entry point is the generic ccol_begin(3) macro, which picks this
function for a cbmap. Call this function directly only when you already
know the container type and want the error string.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cbm   Ordered map to iterate over, or NULL
err   Optional pointer that receives an error string on failure, or
      NULL
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A pointer to a new iterator at the first element.
NULL with *err set to NULL when there is nothing to iterate over, that is
when cbm is NULL or the map holds no elements.
NULL with *err set to an error string when the library can't allocate
the iterator.
.fi
.SH "ITERATION ORDER"
.nf
The walk is an in-order walk of the AVL tree, so it visits the elements
in ascending key order, the same order that the map uses to place a key.
A hash map gives no such order. The order is part of the container's
contract and the caller can depend on it; when the order matters it is
the reason to pick cbmap over chmap.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function sets *err to NULL on entry, before anything else. To tell an
empty result from a failure, test *err rather than the return value.

A NULL cbm iterates as an empty map, with no error reported. This is a
contract shared across modules with chashmap_begin_iter(3) and
cvector_begin_iter(3): a map field can be NULL because nothing has been
put into it yet, and the caller can iterate such a field directly with no
NULL guard of its own.

The iterator frees itself when ccol_iter_next(3) steps past the last
element and returns NULL. A walk that stops early has to end with
ccol_iter_destroy(3), unless the iterator was declared with
ccol_iter_declare(3), which destroys it when the scope exits.

The iterator carries a fixed-size stack for the walk, big enough for the
maximum height an AVL tree of any element count can reach, so creating an
iterator costs one allocation and a step costs none. That allocation goes
through the map's memory management procedures.

Inserting into the map or removing from it during the walk makes the
iterator invalid. Changing a value in place through ccol_iter_val_ptr(3)
doesn't.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cbmap_construct(ages, char *, int);
int age = 30;
cbmap_insert(ages, "alice", age);
age = 41;
cbmap_insert(ages, "bob", age);

char *err = NULL;
ccol_iter_declare(ages, it);
for (it = cbmap_begin_iter(ages, &err); it; it = ccol_iter_next(it)) {
    printf("%s = %d\en", *ccol_iter_key_ptr(it),
                         *ccol_iter_val_ptr(it));
}
if (err) {
    fprintf(stderr, "cannot iterate: %s\en", err);
}
cbmap_destroy(ages);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_begin (3),
.BR ccol_iter_next (3),
.BR ccol_iter_destroy (3),
.BR ccol_iter_declare (3),
.BR chashmap_begin_iter (3),
.BR cvector_begin_iter (3)
